"""Original first-aid and safety drill; first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
First aid is|immediate assistance until further care is available or the situation is resolved|a replacement for all emergency services|a guaranteed diagnosis|only a competition rule|First aid provides immediate assistance and does not replace clinical care. First aid is immediate assistance, not a complete clinical treatment.
A first-aid objective is to|support life and prevent further harm|guarantee recovery in every case|replace all trained care|ignore the scene|First aid aims to support life, prevent further harm and provide reassurance. Reassurance and prevention of further harm are also important aims.
Before approaching a casualty, check|the scene for hazards|only the person's clothing|only the time on a watch|only the colour of equipment|Scene safety helps prevent the rescuer becoming another casualty. Traffic, electricity or fire can create hazards for the rescuer.
A live electrical source is|a hazard that must be addressed before touching the person|safe to touch with bare hands|made safe by pouring water over it|a reason to ignore emergency services|Do not touch someone in contact with a live source; arrange safe disconnection and call for help. A person in contact with live electricity must not be touched until it is safe.
A responder should avoid|entering an unsafe area without protection|calling for emergency assistance|following dispatcher instructions|checking for hazards|Do not enter an unsafe area and become another casualty. Emergency services can coordinate help without exposing another person to danger.
Emergency services should be given|the location, situation and requested information|only a team name|no information about the scene|a textbook title|Accurate location and scene information help emergency responders. Give a precise location and explain hazards where possible.
A bystander should|follow dispatcher instructions and act within training|ignore all instructions|delay calling until a diagnosis is known|force treatment beyond their competence|Dispatcher guidance and appropriate training are important in an emergency. Guidance and training help match the response to the situation.
When a person is able to respond, a first aider should|ask for consent where appropriate|assume consent can never matter|ignore their wishes in all situations|avoid explaining any action|Obtain consent where appropriate and follow applicable local procedures. Communication matters when a person is able to respond.
Protective barriers when available|can reduce exposure to body fluids|guarantee zero risk in every situation|replace the need for scene safety|make hand hygiene irrelevant|Appropriate protective equipment can reduce exposure but does not eliminate all risk. Appropriate protective equipment complements scene safety.
An unresponsive person who is not breathing normally|needs emergency help and dispatcher-guided CPR|should be forced to drink|should be left alone without calling|can wait for a textbook diagnosis|Unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing require urgent help and CPR guidance. Do not delay calling while trying to make a diagnosis.
An AED is|a device that analyses heart rhythm and gives prompts when used|a method of measuring food portions|a substitute for calling emergency services|a device that treats every illness|An automated external defibrillator analyses rhythm and provides instructions. The device provides prompts to guide its use.
If an AED is available during a suspected cardiac emergency|turn it on and follow its prompts while emergency help is arranged|ignore its instructions|delay calling for help|use it only after obtaining a textbook diagnosis|Follow AED prompts and emergency dispatcher guidance. Follow the device and dispatcher instructions while help is arranged.
CPR procedures|can differ by age and situation and require appropriate training|are identical in every circumstance without exception|replace all emergency services|are a method of diagnosing malaria|CPR techniques can vary; training and local guidance matter. Age and situation may affect appropriate CPR procedures.
An unresponsive person who is breathing normally|needs monitoring and appropriate emergency assistance|should be forced to eat|can always be left alone|must immediately stand and walk|Monitor breathing, call for help and follow dispatcher guidance. Breathing may change, so continued monitoring matters.
A recovery position may|help maintain the airway when appropriate|be used instead of CPR for a person not breathing normally|replace all monitoring|guarantee that breathing cannot change|A recovery position can help when an unresponsive person breathes normally, subject to circumstances and guidance. Recovery position is not a substitute for CPR when normal breathing is absent.
If spinal injury is suspected|avoid unnecessary movement and follow dispatcher guidance|force the person to sit up|twist the spine to test movement|ignore breathing|Avoid unnecessary movement unless needed for safety or essential care; follow emergency guidance.
Why must food or drink not be forced into an unresponsive person?|because it can be dangerous|because monitoring must be avoided|because emergency services should not be called|because training is never relevant|Forcing anything into the mouth of an unresponsive person can be dangerous.
Severe external bleeding requires|urgent help and firm direct pressure when safe and appropriate|waiting without monitoring|forcing the person to continue activity|ignoring the wound|Call for help and apply appropriate direct pressure to severe bleeding.
When an object is embedded in a wound|do not remove it; apply pressure around it and seek help|pull it out immediately|press directly on it|ignore severe bleeding|Removing an embedded object can worsen bleeding; pressure is applied around it.
A tourniquet may be used for|life-threatening limb bleeding according to training and local guidance|every minor scratch|replacing all emergency help|measuring body temperature|A tourniquet can be appropriate for life-threatening limb bleeding when used according to training.
Direct pressure for bleeding should be|firm and maintained as directed|applied only after all bleeding stops|used to force an embedded object deeper|a substitute for emergency help in severe cases|Firm pressure can help control severe bleeding while emergency care is arranged.
For a thermal burn, a suitable initial action is to|cool the area with cool running water when safe and feasible|apply ice directly|apply butter|pull away material stuck to skin|Cool running water is recommended for thermal burns; avoid ice or unverified substances.
A thermal burn is commonly cooled under running water for|20 minutes where possible|20 seconds only in every case|no time at all|20 hours without assessment|NHS guidance recommends cooling a thermal burn under cool running water for 20 minutes where possible.
While cooling a burn, the responder should|keep the rest of the person warm|cool the whole person until shivering|ignore scene safety|apply ice to the wound|Cool the burn while keeping the rest of the person warm.
Clothing stuck to a burn should|not be pulled away|always be pulled off immediately|be removed with force|be ignored while ice is applied|Do not pull away material stuck to burned skin.
Large, deep, electrical or chemical burns|need urgent assessment and situation-specific guidance|are always safe to ignore|are treated identically without considering the hazard|can be diagnosed from colour alone|Serious burns need urgent assessment and guidance suited to the cause.
A suspected fracture should generally be|kept still while appropriate help is arranged|forced straight to test movement|massaged strongly|ignored until the next day in every case|Keep the injured area still and obtain appropriate assessment.
A protruding bone should|not be pushed back|be forced into place|be moved repeatedly to test it|be treated as harmless|Do not push a protruding bone back; seek appropriate emergency care.
A suspected head, neck or spinal injury calls for|avoiding unnecessary movement and following emergency guidance|twisting the person to test the spine|forcing the person to stand|ignoring airway and breathing|Avoid unnecessary movement unless required for safety or essential care.
Scene safety and urgent care|must both be considered|mean a responder should enter any hazard|make emergency services unnecessary|replace all training|Protect the responder while arranging urgent help and following guidance.
Storing medicines and chemicals securely|can reduce accidental exposure|guarantees no incident is possible|makes labels unnecessary|replaces all supervision|Secure, labelled storage is one part of prevention.
Chemicals should not be placed in food or drink containers because|they may be mistaken for something safe to consume|labels have no purpose|all chemicals are harmless|storage never affects safety|Misleading containers can lead to accidental exposure.
A first-aid kit|is useful but does not replace training or emergency services|guarantees successful treatment|diagnoses every injury|eliminates the need for a safety plan|A kit supports trained response but is not a substitute for care.
A school emergency plan should identify|who calls for help, the location and available equipment|only the colour of a poster|only the school's sports score|no role for accessibility|A practical plan defines responsibilities, location, equipment and access needs.
Local emergency numbers should be|verified in advance for the setting|invented from memory without checking|treated as identical everywhere|ignored in a severe emergency|Emergency arrangements differ; verify the appropriate number locally.
If breathing changes while awaiting help|inform the dispatcher and follow instructions|assume the change never matters|force food into the mouth|stop monitoring entirely|Breathing can change and the dispatcher should be updated.
First-aid instructions depend on|the condition, responder training and local guidance|one universal action for every injury|only the casualty's name|only the event's score|Different conditions require different responses and guidance.
An emergency responder should|reassure the person and monitor their condition when safe|promise a guaranteed outcome|leave a severe casualty without help|ignore changing symptoms|Reassurance and monitoring can support care while help is arranged.
A burn, a bleeding wound and a fracture|need responses matched to the situation|must all receive identical treatment|never need emergency assessment|are diagnosed only by the same symptom|First aid depends on the injury and circumstances.
A textbook about first aid|cannot replace hands-on training or dispatcher guidance|guarantees competence in every emergency|replaces all emergency services|establishes a diagnosis without assessment|Practical training and local emergency instructions are essential.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
